%I A396699 #23 Sep 14 2026 22:20:50
%S A396699 1,2,5,10,18,28,43,60,84,110,145,182,230,280,343,408,488,570,669,770,
%T A396699 890,1012,1155,1300,1468,1638,1833,2030,2254,2480,2735,2992,3280,3570,
%U A396699 3893,4218,4578,4940,5339,5740,6180,6622,7105,7590,8118,8648,9223,9800,10424
%N A396699 a(2) = 1; a(2*m) = m*(2*m^2+1)/3 - 1 for m >= 2, and a(2*m+1) = m*(m+1)*(2*m+1)/3 for m >= 1.
%C A396699 This sequence counts the permutations p(1),...,p(n) of 1,...,n that satisfy all three conditions below.
%C A396699 (i) The entry 1 is not first. Write r for its position, so p(r) = 1 and r >= 2. The pair considered is p(r-1),p(r), namely 1 and the entry immediately before it. That entry is necessarily larger than 1.
%C A396699 (ii) No segment of fewer than n consecutive positions containing both positions r-1 and r has a set of consecutive integer values. The values need not occur in numerical order. Equivalently, for 1 <= i < r <= j <= n with j-i+1 < n, max(p(i),...,p(j)) - min(p(i),...,p(j)) != j-i.
%C A396699 (iii) It is possible to color each entry red or blue, using both colors, so that the entries of each color increase from left to right. For two successive entries of the same color, their positions differ by 1 or 2, and their values also differ by 1 or 2. A color may occur just once, in which case it has no successive pair to check.
%C A396699 Each permutation is counted once, regardless of how many colorings satisfy (iii). There is no additional choice of a marked pair: its position is determined by the position of 1.
%C A396699 Cowan (2026) calls such a permutation, with the pair p(r-1),p(r) distinguished, a descending rooted lens block. The linked paper relates these objects to the Ray-West correction statistic j(pi) (Ray and West, 2003, Theorem 8.7) and Problem 3.7 of Vatter (2026).
%C A396699 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of pairs of binary words (X,Y) of length n with X = 1^a (01)^(u-1) 0^b and Y = 0^c (10)^(v-1) 1^d, where a,b,c,d,u,v >= 1, c = b+u-v, d = a+u-v, and every proper prefix of X contains strictly more 1's than the same-length prefix of Y (with equality for the full words).
%C A396699 Interleaves the octahedral numbers minus one (even n, except a(2) = 1) with twice the square pyramidal numbers (odd n).
%H A396699 Eric Cowan, <a href="/A396699/b396699.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..1000</a>
%H A396699 Eric Cowan, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22004734">A lens-block interpretation of the Ray-West statistic</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A396699 N. Ray and J. West, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s000260300005">Posets of matrices and permutations with forbidden subsequences</a>, Ann. Comb. 7 (2003), no. 1, 55-88. (<a href="https://eprints.maths.manchester.ac.uk/609/1/lume2wjganftta0j.pdf">Author version</a>.)
%H A396699 V. Vatter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16355">An assortment of problems in permutation patterns: unimodality, equivalence, derangements, and sorting</a>, arXiv:2602.16355 [math.CO], 2026. See Problem 3.7.
%H A396699 <a href="/index/Rec#order_06">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (2,1,-4,1,2,-1).
%F A396699 a(2) = 1; a(2*m) = m*(2*m^2+1)/3 - 1 = A005900(m) - 1 for m >= 2; a(2*m+1) = m*(m+1)*(2*m+1)/3 = A006331(m) for m >= 1.
%F A396699 G.f.: x^2*(1 + 2*x^3 - 2*x^5 + x^6)/((1-x)^4*(1+x)^2).
%F A396699 a(n) = 2*a(n-1) + a(n-2) - 4*a(n-3) + a(n-4) + 2*a(n-5) - a(n-6) for n >= 9.
%F A396699 a(n) = Sum_{u=1..floor(n/2)} Sum_{k=1..n-2*u+1} (u + min(k, n-2*u+2-k) - 1) - [n even and n >= 4], where [.] = 1 if the condition holds and 0 otherwise.
%e A396699 For n = 2, only 21 satisfies the conditions, so a(2) = 1. Color 2 red and 1 blue; there is no shorter segment containing both entries.
%e A396699 For n = 3, the two permutations are 231 and 312, so a(3) = 2. In each, the entry immediately before 1 is 3. The only shorter segment containing this pair has values {1,3}, which are not consecutive integers. For 231, color the entries 2,3 red and 1 blue; for 312, color 3 red and 1,2 blue. The two entries of the repeated color differ by 1 in both position and value.
%e A396699 The permutations 123 and 132 fail (i); 213 fails (ii) because its first two entries have values {1,2}; 321 fails (ii) because its last two entries have values {1,2}, and also fails (iii).
%e A396699 For n = 4, the five permutations are 2341, 2413, 3142, 3412, and 4123, so a(4) = 5. For example, in 2413 color 2,4 red and 1,3 blue: successive entries of each color differ by 1 in position and 2 in value. The shorter segments containing the adjacent entries 4,1 have value sets {1,4}, {1,2,4}, and {1,3,4}, none of which consists of consecutive integers.
%o A396699 (Python)
%o A396699 def a(n):
%o A396699     if n == 2: return 1
%o A396699     m = n//2
%o A396699     return m*(2*m*m+1)//3 - 1 if n%2 == 0 else m*(m+1)*(2*m+1)//3
%o A396699 (PARI) a(n) = if(n==2, 1, my(m=n\2); if(n%2==0, m*(2*m^2+1)/3-1, m*(m+1)*(2*m+1)/3))
%Y A396699 Cf. A005900, A006331.
